# ── MLT staging environment ───────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Copy to `.env.staging`, fill in, and push it with:
#     .\deploy\deploy.ps1 -Server user@host -Key ~\.ssh\key -Env .env.staging
#
# `.env.staging` is gitignored. This example file carries NO secrets and is committed
# so the required shape is documented.
#
# One file serves three consumers, which is why it looks like more than it is:
#   * docker-compose interpolation  → IMAGE_REF, STAGING_DOMAIN
#   * the API container's env       → everything else         (runtime)
#   * deploy.ps1's preflight        → cross-checks the two client IDs match

# ── Deployment identity ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# The public hostname. Caddy requests a Let's Encrypt certificate for exactly this name,
# so it must already resolve to this server before the first deploy. The instance runs
# WITHOUT an Elastic IP and takes a new public address on every start, so DuckDNS (the
# @reboot updater from bootstrap.sh) is what keeps this name pointing at it.
STAGING_DOMAIN=mlt-staging.duckdns.org

# The image to run. Built by .github/workflows/image.yml and pushed to ECR — this box
# never builds anything, which is why it needs no source and no repo credential.
#
# deploy.ps1 also derives the ECR REGISTRY from this (everything before the first '/')
# when it runs bootstrap.sh, so it must be the full ref, not a bare repository name.
# Pin a specific build with `deploy.ps1 -Tag <git-sha>` rather than editing this.
IMAGE_REF=123456789012.dkr.ecr.ap-south-1.amazonaws.com/mlt-api:latest

# ── CLIProxyAPI (optional second container) ───────────────────────────────────
# github.com/router-for-me/CLIProxyAPI — an OpenAI-compatible front end for provider CLI
# subscriptions, feeding the `local` provider in src/rag_pipeline.py.
#
# COMPOSE_PROFILES is the on/off switch, and it is read by docker compose itself: leave it
# unset and the cliproxy service is not created, so this stays the two-container stack it
# has always been. Set it and THREE things must line up, or deploy.ps1 stops you:
#   COMPOSE_PROFILES=cliproxy, CLIPROXY_IMAGE_REF, and deploy/cliproxy.config.yaml.
#
# A SECOND ECR repository, not another tag in the first: the lifecycle policy there keeps
# only the latest 2 images, so MLT builds would evict the proxy. Built and pushed from
# Windows by deploy/cliproxy.ps1 (upstream is a third-party repo; CI does not build it).
# COMPOSE_PROFILES=cliproxy
# CLIPROXY_IMAGE_REF=123456789012.dkr.ecr.ap-south-1.amazonaws.com/iitm/ailab/cliproxy:latest

# BAKED INTO THE JS BUNDLE AT BUILD TIME — Vite substitutes import.meta.env.VITE_* when
# the image is built, so changing either of these requires a REBUILD, not a restart.
#
# Setting them HERE does not build anything. The build happens in GitHub Actions from the
# repository VARIABLES of the same names; these two copies exist so update.sh can compare
# them against the image's OCI labels and refuse to start an image that was built for a
# different domain or client ID. Change the repository variable, re-run the workflow, and
# keep this file in step — update.sh fails loudly if they drift.
#
# VITE_API_URL must be the full https:// origin. Leaving it EMPTY does not mean
# "same origin": web/src/api/client.js:1 uses `|| 'http://localhost:8000'`, so an empty
# value ships a bundle that calls localhost. deploy.ps1 refuses to deploy if it is not https://.
VITE_API_URL=https://mlt-staging.duckdns.org
VITE_GOOGLE_CLIENT_ID=000000000000-xxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxx.apps.googleusercontent.com

# ── Google sign-in (the only way into the app) ────────────────────────────────
# MUST be byte-identical to VITE_GOOGLE_CLIENT_ID above, or Google's `aud` check rejects
# every login. deploy.ps1 hard-fails on a mismatch rather than letting you find out at
# the login screen.
GOOGLE_CLIENT_ID=000000000000-xxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxx.apps.googleusercontent.com

# A NEW long random string — do not reuse the local one. Unset ⇒ every auth endpoint
# 503s. Changing it invalidates every issued session token (7-day TTL, no refresh
# endpoint), which logs everyone out.
#   PowerShell:  -join ((48..57)+(65..90)+(97..122) | Get-Random -Count 64 | % {[char]$_})
JWT_SECRET=

# Restricts who may sign in, checked against Google's `hd` claim first and the email
# suffix as fallback. EMPTY MEANS ANY GOOGLE ACCOUNT ON THE INTERNET — fine on localhost,
# not fine on a public URL. Comma-separated.
ALLOWED_EMAIL_DOMAINS=ds.study.iitm.ac.in

# Comma-separated. These addresses get is_admin at each login AND bypass the domain
# restriction above, which is how a gmail.com owner still administers an
# institute-domain-restricted instance. Removing an address here revokes admin at that
# user's next sign-in — no DB surgery.
ADMIN_EMAILS=jibzmat@gmail.com

# Read ONCE at import, so a change needs an API restart, not just a new request. Largely
# moot in this deployment because FastAPI serves the SPA from the same origin — set
# correctly anyway so a future split frontend does not fail mysteriously.
CORS_ORIGINS=https://mlt-staging.duckdns.org

# ── Vector store (Qdrant Cloud — already provisioned, reachable from anywhere) ─
# The `mlt_course_bot` collection must already exist: the API opens it with
# from_existing_collection, which does not create. `mlt_question_units` IS auto-created.
QDRANT_URL=https://xxxxxxxx-xxxx-xxxx-xxxx-xxxxxxxxxxxx.us-west-2-0.aws.cloud.qdrant.io
QDRANT_API_KEY=

# ── LLM providers ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# At least one key is required. With none, the API does NOT error — answer_question
# returns HTTP 200 carrying raw retrieved excerpts and provider_used="none (all providers
# failed)". A broken deployment therefore looks healthy; check that field when smoke-testing.
LLM_PROVIDER=gemini
GOOGLE_API_KEY=
GROQ_API_KEY=

# Extra keys for the rotation pool are optional and use these exact names:
#   GEMINI_API_KEY_1..20 / GOOGLE_API_KEY_1..20 / GROQ_API_KEY_1..20
# ⚠️ NEVER add a variable named api1..api20, API_1.., api_1.. or groq_1.. to this file.
# rag_pipeline.py:120-127 sweeps those names up and posts their VALUES to Groq as API
# keys, with no format validation. deploy.ps1 refuses to deploy a file containing one.

# ── Cross-encoder reranker (optional second instance) ─────────────────────────
# Provisioned by `terraform apply` in infra/reranker/, which prints both values below —
# `terraform output env_line` gives the first verbatim.
#
# The reranker is a SEPARATE EC2 instance, not another container in this compose stack.
# That is the entire point: this box is a t3.micro with ~1 GiB of RAM and cannot hold a
# second model in-process. The reranker box is a t3.small in the same VPC.
#
# Use the PRIVATE IP. The reranker's security group admits this instance's security group
# and nothing else, so there is no public route to it — which is also why an Elastic IP is
# irrelevant here: the private address is stable for the instance's lifetime, unlike this
# box's public one (see STAGING_DOMAIN above).
#
# Setting these does NOT switch reranking on. That is a runtime toggle in the admin panel
# under Retrieval, persisted in app_settings, so it survives a redeploy and can be turned
# off during an incident without touching this file. Use "Test connection" there first —
# it probes /health and reports the latency.
# RERANKER_URL=http://10.0.1.42:8080
# RERANKER_API_KEY=

# Candidates pulled from Qdrant per chat question. 10 is the long-standing default and
# what ships; ~20 gives the reranker a wider field to choose from at the cost of a few ms.
# CHAT_RETRIEVAL_K=10

# ── Deliberately unset on staging ─────────────────────────────────────────────
# ADMIN_TOKEN
#   An identity-less shared secret that grants the entire admin surface via a header CORS
#   explicitly allows. Anyone holding it is every admin, with no audit trail. ADMIN_EMAILS
#   above covers admin properly through the normal bearer token, so leave this unset.
#
# LOCAL_LLM_BASE_URL — unset UNLESS you enabled the cliproxy profile above.
#   Your local .env probably points this at a machine on your LAN, which the server cannot
#   reach. Harmless while LLM_PROVIDER=gemini (it is last in the queue) but it costs a
#   failed attempt plus LOCAL_LLM_TIMEOUT seconds on every failover walk, so deploy.ps1
#   warns when it is set without cliproxy.
#
#   With cliproxy running, this is how the API reaches it. The hostname must be the compose
#   SERVICE NAME: inside the api container, localhost is the api container, so
#   http://localhost:8317 is the single most likely way to get this wrong — deploy.ps1
#   hard-fails on it. LOCAL_LLM_API_KEYS must match one of the api-keys in
#   deploy/cliproxy.config.yaml, and LOCAL_LLM_MODELS must name models the proxy actually
#   serves (there is no catalogue; whatever you authenticated is what works).
# LOCAL_LLM_BASE_URL=http://cliproxy:8317/v1
# LOCAL_LLM_API_KEYS=the-same-long-random-string-as-cliproxy.config.yaml
# LOCAL_LLM_MODELS=gpt-5,claude-sonnet-4-5
#
#   Leave LLM_PROVIDER=gemini to keep the proxy as a last-resort fallback, or set
#   LLM_PROVIDER=local to make it the primary and the hosted keys the fallback.
